<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">The Library</p>
  <h1>Further Research</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">The reading list, with its sources visible. Check every one of them.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p>This is the spine of EGregoRA. Nothing said on the podcast, drawn in a plate or printed in the codex is
    meant to be taken on authority — including from Ed. The list below is sorted by limb and marked by
    <strong class="muted">evidential weight</strong>, so you always know whether you are holding peer-reviewed
    science, serious scholarship, contested speculation or frank mythology. All four are worth reading.
    Confusing them is the only sin.</p>
    <p class="muted small legend">
      <span class="sci">◆ Established science</span> ·
      <span class="hist">◆ Scholarship &amp; history</span> ·
      <span class="spec">◆ Speculative / contested</span> ·
      <span class="myth">◆ Myth, tradition &amp; primary esoteric text</span>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Cosmic Physics &amp; Astronomy</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Carlo Rovelli — <em>The Order of Time</em>; <em>Reality Is Not What It Seems</em>.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Sean Carroll — <em>The Big Picture</em>; <em>From Eternity to Here</em> (entropy and the arrow of time).</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Leonard Susskind — <em>The Black Hole War</em> (information, horizons, holography).</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Jim Al-Khalili &amp; Johnjoe McFadden — <em>Life on the Edge</em> (quantum biology, carefully).</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> NASA ADS, arXiv (astro-ph) and the ESA Gaia archive — the primary literature, free.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Trees, Druidry &amp; the Living Earth</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Suzanne Simard — <em>Finding the Mother Tree</em> (mycorrhizal networks, from the researcher herself).</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Merlin Sheldrake — <em>Entangled Life</em>.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Robin Wall Kimmerer — <em>Braiding Sweetgrass</em> (indigenous knowledge and botany, held together).</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Ronald Hutton — <em>Blood and Mistletoe: The History of the Druids in Britain</em>. The indispensable corrective.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> Robert Graves — <em>The White Goddess</em>. Magnificent, enormously influential, and historically unreliable. Read it as poetry.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Sacred Geometry &amp; Natural Form</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> D'Arcy Wentworth Thompson — <em>On Growth and Form</em> (1917). Still the foundation.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Philip Ball — <em>Patterns in Nature</em>; <em>The Self-Made Tapestry</em>.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> H. S. M. Coxeter — <em>Regular Polytopes</em> (the real mathematics of the solids).</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> Keith Critchlow — <em>Order in Space</em>. Beautiful; treat its metaphysical leaps as claims, not conclusions.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Mario Livio — <em>The Golden Ratio</em>, which usefully debunks half of what you have been told about φ.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Neuroscience, Psychology &amp; the Visionary State</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Anil Seth — <em>Being You</em> (predictive processing and the controlled hallucination).</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Carhart-Harris &amp; Friston — “REBUS and the Anarchic Brain” (<em>Pharmacological Reviews</em>, 2019).</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Michael Pollan — <em>How to Change Your Mind</em>; and Griffiths et al., Johns Hopkins psilocybin trials.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> William James — <em>The Varieties of Religious Experience</em> (1902). Still unsurpassed.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> C. G. Jung — <em>Memories, Dreams, Reflections</em>; <em>Aion</em>. Psychology on the border of myth.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Daniel Kahneman — <em>Thinking, Fast and Slow</em>; Keith Stanovich on dysrationalia. Know your own failure modes.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Biology &amp; the Stubbornness of Life</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Lynn Margulis — <em>Symbiotic Planet</em> (symbiogenesis; she was right and was mocked for it).</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Nick Lane — <em>The Vital Question</em>; <em>Transformer</em> (energy, origins, metabolism).</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Michael Levin — bioelectricity and morphogenesis; his open-access papers are the frontier.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Maturana &amp; Varela — <em>The Tree of Knowledge</em> (autopoiesis; where biology meets epistemology).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Astrology — History &amp; Honest Assessment</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Nicholas Campion — <em>A History of Western Astrology</em> (2 vols). The scholarly standard.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> Ptolemy — <em>Tetrabiblos</em>; Vettius Valens — <em>Anthologies</em>. The primary sources.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Shawn Carlson, “A double-blind test of astrology” (<em>Nature</em>, 1985) — and the debate that followed it.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Geoffrey Dean's meta-analyses. Read these before you defend the predictive claim.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>God, the Law of One &amp; the Contact Question</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> Elkins, Rueckert &amp; McCarty — <em>The Ra Material / The Law of One</em>, Books I–V. The primary text; read it as philosophy.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Plotinus — <em>The Enneads</em>; Shankara on Advaita; Meister Eckhart's sermons. The same intuition, three cultures.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Alfred North Whitehead — <em>Process and Reality</em>. The hardest book on this list and the most rewarding.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> Avi Loeb — <em>Extraterrestrial</em>; and the Galileo Project papers. Contested, but properly argued.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Jacques Vallée — <em>Passport to Magonia</em>. The UFO phenomenon read as folklore, which may be the most useful lens of all.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> The 2021 and 2023 US ODNI UAP reports — the actual official record, rather than what people say is in it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Magic, Wizards &amp; Symbolic Practice</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Frances Yates — <em>The Art of Memory</em>; <em>Giordano Bruno and the Hermetic Tradition</em>. Essential.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Ronald Hutton — <em>The Triumph of the Moon</em> (where modern pagan magic actually came from).</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <em>The Corpus Hermeticum</em>; Agrippa — <em>Three Books of Occult Philosophy</em>. The primary sources, in their own voice.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> Alan Moore and Grant Morrison on magic-as-fiction-as-magic. Unserious in tone, serious in insight.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Ellen Langer and the placebo/expectancy literature — the measurable edge of ritual.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Alchemy &amp; the Elemental Forces</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Lawrence Principe — <em>The Secrets of Alchemy</em>. The best modern account; he reproduces the experiments.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> William Newman — <em>Atoms and Alchemy</em>; Newman &amp; Principe on Starkey and Boyle. Where chymistry becomes chemistry.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> The <em>Emerald Tablet</em>; Maier's <em>Atalanta Fugiens</em> (1617); the <em>Mutus Liber</em>. Primary sources — read as emblem, not instruction.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> C. G. Jung — <em>Psychology and Alchemy</em>; <em>Mysterium Coniunctionis</em>. The opus as a map of individuation.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Peter Atkins — <em>The Four Laws That Drive the Universe</em>; and any good text on phase transitions. The elements, rendered as states and energetics.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Gaston Bachelard — <em>The Psychoanalysis of Fire</em>; <em>Water and Dreams</em>. Why the four still think so well.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>The Shadow Cabinet — Occultism, Propaganda &amp; Fascism</h2>
      <p class="muted small legend">Studied as pathology. These are works of history and warning.</p>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Nicholas Goodrick-Clarke — <em>The Occult Roots of Nazism</em>. The definitive, sober account; it deflates as many myths as it confirms.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Heather Pringle — <em>The Master Plan: Himmler's Scholars and the Holocaust</em> (the Ahnenerbe).</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Eric Kurlander — <em>Hitler's Monsters: A Supernatural History of the Third Reich</em>.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Primo Levi — <em>If This Is a Man</em>; Hannah Arendt — <em>The Origins of Totalitarianism</em>. Never study the symbols without the consequences.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> Umberto Eco — “Ur-Fascism” (1995). Fourteen warning signs; learn them by heart.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> The USC Shoah Foundation and Yad Vashem archives — primary testimony, freely available.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

