<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2>How to Read Like a Member of This Order</h2>
    <details class="scroll" open><summary>1. Separate the claim from the feeling it gives you</summary>
      <p>Awe is evidence that you are alive, not that a statement is true. Enjoy the awe; audit the claim separately.</p></details>
    <details class="scroll"><summary>2. Ask what would have to be false</summary>
      <p>Any idea worth holding can tell you what observation would overturn it. Ideas that cannot are not knowledge — they may still be beautiful, but file them under myth.</p></details>
    <details class="scroll"><summary>3. Find the best critic, not the worst</summary>
      <p>Read the strongest opponent of the thing you love. If you cannot state their case in their own words, you do not yet understand your own.</p></details>
    <details class="scroll"><summary>4. Follow the citation to its source</summary>
      <p>Most esoteric "facts" are a chain of books quoting books. Walk it back to the primary document. Roughly half the chains end in nothing at all.</p></details>
    <details class="scroll"><summary>5. Keep the two ledgers open</summary>
      <p>One for what is demonstrated, one for what is meaningful. Let them inform each other. Never let one forge entries in the other.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>
